[{:seon.task/name "a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: \"Slow tests should be treated as bugs and fixed\"; every process test pays a root's boot and its first agent. Profiled 2026-11-08 (load 16-27, so inflated; the ratios hold): before the boot fork server serves its first :fork it decodes all of main's indexes (seon.db/preload!, 3.2-4.7 s), builds main's reach graph and reconciler index (server-step's init, 3.2-3.9 s), compiles main's tests (load-all!, 2-3 s; now at its start), builds the wiki's FTS5 text index and warms an agent's first write (warm-start, 2.2-3.1 s), and freezes the heap (static!, 0.4-0.7 s): about 70% of a first agent's wait, and a scripted test agent uses almost none of it. Minimum: a fork costs a fork (~70 ms). Plan: the boot server forks agents at once and warms in steps between requests (each a message to itself: indexes, graph, index, text index, tests by namespace), and an agent forked before a piece is warm makes that piece itself when it first uses it (base-graph, reconcile-index, text search, its prove's test load), as derived data regenerable from the db at t (docs/data-modeling.md#derived-data-is-a-cache-never-relied-on); a later fork inherits it warm. Also read the reach graph from the kept program store where bin/test keeps it (test-runner/reach-graph-of: a sixth of a build's ticks) rather than building it. Proofs: a cost proof that a root's first agent is ready within a fork's work plus its own start, flat in the program's size; seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them still green (an agent that runs tests finds them, loading them itself when it came first). Wiki: docs/machine.md#an-agents-first-write."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 by the stability sweep (docs/failure-points.md), checked against the code; a lost server's waits are answered already (`seon.fork-server/fork-server-proc`'s :lost answers its :waiters). Both parts read true on 2026-11-06. (1) :stats and :static answers pair with :waiters by FIFO (`peek`). After a promote the standby is sent every unanswered request again, so FIFO holds only while every answer the dead server wrote arrived whole before its EOF; an answer cut by the death pairs the next waiter with the wrong request. The request carries its :rid to the server and the answer echoes it, and the proc answers the waiter by :rid. (2) `seon.fork-server/release!` waits for the boot server's exit with a blocking `c-waitpid` (no WNOHANG), so a wedged server (a deadlocked child, docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads) hangs root's stop; then `.join 5000` on the event reader is a clock deciding (§4). Stop is a kill of the server's process group (as a root's processes end with it, docs/machine.md#processes) followed by the reap and the reader's EOF, and no timeout. Deletes the join's timeout; replaces FIFO pairing with :rid pairing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-fork-servers-idle-wait-is-two-parks"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the blocking-calls audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md; item 25). What exists: the fork server waits for root and its children in one kqueue call, seon.lib/kq-wait, with no timeout and :blocking, so it is correct only while the server hosts no other fiber, and lib.clj kq-new/kq-watch/kq-wait and native/probe.c are kqueue only. Correct: root's socket is poller/wait-ready fd :read and a child exit is the poller child-exit wait (jolt.io-poller/a-fiber-waits-for-a-childs-exit); kq-new, kq-watch, kq-wait and the adopt path go. Needs seon.sock/a-send-parks-like-a-receive for its event sends. Proof to write: the server answers a frame while a child exit and a send are pending, on one carrier."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tx-step-that-threw-on-a-map-says-which"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen once 2026-11-05 in the standing store's problems before its erase, not reproduced: the fork server's step threw on :tx, nth not supported on this type: PersistentArrayMap. The likely site, by reading only: seon.fork-server/serve's :tx arm takes (nth % 2 nil) of each of the message's :tx-data, which assumes a datom or a vector; an entry that is a map throws. The step's failure answers and the server serves on, so what is lost is that commit's application to this server's main until the next. Correct: find which writer sends a map in :tx-data, and make the message's shape a contract so the class is refused where it is made. Proof to write: the step given a commit whose tx-data the writer really sends."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
{:seon.task/name "an-agent-inherits-the-section-leaves-it-searches"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-08 (an agent's first search, seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited): its first grep allocated 8-15 MB more than its second, all cold decoding of the EAVT leaves holding the sections' :seon.doc/text, which every forked agent decodes for itself; the text index itself is inherited current. Candidate: read those leaves in seon.fork-server/warm-start, so each agent inherits them decoded (copy-on-write). Measure first: the fork server's resident memory with and without it (docs/machine.md's budget), against the bytes saved per agent times agents per hour; propose to the owner with the numbers. Not started."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-source-root-with-no-deps-edn-starts-the-fork-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 (the audit fixes' helper): seon.fork-server/dev-classpath! slurps the source root's deps.edn with nothing named for its absence, so a source root with none (a scratch tree a test boots, a user's repository that is not a Clojure project) throws at the fork server's start and root reports the fork server lost; seon.root-test's boots-on-a-tree writes {:deps {}} to get past it. Correct: a root with no deps.edn has no dependency roots to add (absent is no deps, a fact of the tree, not a fake default), and a deps.edn that does not read is a typed error named in docs/failure-points.md, answered to whoever asked for the fork. Delete the {:deps {}} line from boots-on-a-tree. Proof, red first: a boot on a tree with no deps.edn spawns an agent."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
